MVM.registerModule({
  "id": "m08",
  "summary": "Explain how the engine computer uses sensors and actuators to control the engine, how emissions form and are reduced, and how to read trouble codes and live data with a scan tool.",
  "outcomes": ["Describe the inputs, processing and outputs of an engine control unit, and open and closed loop operation.","Name the main engine sensors, what each measures and what a failed sensor causes.","Describe the main actuators and how to test an actuator.","Explain how pollutants form and how the catalytic converter, EGR, DPF, SCR and evaporative systems reduce them.","Read the structure of a generic OBD-II code, and use freeze-frame and live data.","Diagnose common emission and management faults and explain why removing emission parts is illegal and harmful."],
  "cheatsheet": ["Engine control: sensors (inputs) → computer (processing) → actuators (outputs).","Closed loop: the computer corrects the mixture using the oxygen sensor. Open loop: it uses stored values.","A diagnostic trouble code points to a circuit or system, not automatically to a faulty part.","OBD-II codes: P = powertrain, B = body, C = chassis, U = network. P0xxx is a generic code.","A three-way catalytic converter reduces CO, HC and NOx at near-ideal mixture (lambda 1).","A misfire can overheat and destroy a catalytic converter because unburnt fuel burns in it.","Removing a catalyst, DPF or EGR is illegal in most countries and increases harmful pollution.","Long and short-term fuel trim show how much the computer is correcting the mixture.","Read the live data and codes before you replace any sensor."],
  "lessons": [
    {"id": "m08-l1", "title": "Engine control basics", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Modern engines are controlled by a computer. It reads the sensors, decides how much fuel and spark the engine needs, and operates the actuators. This lesson explains the principles."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"engine-management-loop"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Inputs, processing and outputs"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The computer is called the **engine control unit (ECU)**, the **engine control module (ECM)** or the **powertrain control module (PCM)** if it also controls the transmission. It works in three steps."},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Inputs:** sensors measure engine speed, air quantity, throttle position, temperature, exhaust oxygen and more, and send voltage signals to the ECU.","**Processing:** the ECU compares the signals with stored maps and calculates the fuel quantity, spark timing and other outputs.","**Outputs:** the ECU switches actuators such as injectors, ignition coils, the idle control and the EGR valve."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Open and closed loop"},
      {"t":"p","text":"When the engine is cold, the oxygen sensor is not yet working, so the ECU uses stored values to set the fuel. This is **open loop**. When the engine warms up and the oxygen sensor is ready, the ECU compares the sensor signal with the target and adjusts the fuel to hold the mixture at the right value. This is **closed loop**, because the output affects the input, which is checked again and again."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Adaptive learning"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Parts wear and conditions change. The ECU stores corrections, called **adaptations** or long-term trims, so that the engine keeps running well as parts age. After you replace a sensor or throttle body, or disconnect the battery, the ECU may need to relearn. Follow the manual for the procedure."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Limp mode"},
      {"t":"p","text":"If a critical sensor fails, the ECU may use a fixed default value and limit engine power to protect the engine. This is called **limp mode** or fail-safe mode. A warning lamp comes on, and the fault is stored as a code."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Inside the control unit"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An ECU is a small computer in a sealed case. A **microprocessor** runs the program. **Read-only memory** (flash) holds the program and the fuel and ignition maps. **Random-access memory** holds the readings it is working with, and **non-volatile memory** keeps learned values, fault codes and the odometer, even with the power off. **Input circuits** turn sensor signals into numbers the processor can read, and **driver circuits** switch the injectors, coils and relays."},
      {"t":"ul","items":["The ECU needs a good **battery supply**, a **switched supply** from the ignition and several **earth** connections. A bad earth or low voltage can cause strange faults and false codes.","The ECU shares data with other modules over the vehicle network (module 12), for example the gearbox, ABS and instrument cluster.","Manufacturers release **software updates** to fix faults and improve performance. They are loaded through the diagnostic socket with approved equipment. Never interrupt an update, and keep the battery supply steady while it runs.","The ECU is linked to the **immobiliser**. A replacement ECU usually has to be coded to the vehicle's keys and to its VIN."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Fail-safe strategies"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Failed input","Typical default the ECU uses","What the driver notices"],"rows":[["Coolant temperature","A fixed value, such as 80 °C, or a value based on air temperature","Fan always on, hard hot start, poor economy"],["Air temperature","A fixed value","Little or no change in running"],["MAF sensor","Calculates air from throttle and speed","Poor power and hesitation, with a code"],["Throttle or pedal sensor","Limited power, limp mode, a fixed idle","Engine response is dull"],["Crankshaft sensor","Falls back to the camshaft signal, or the engine stops","Long cranking or stalling"]],"caption":"Fail-safe values keep the car moving. They also hide the fault, so always read the codes."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"The ECU can only act on what the sensors tell it. A bad signal gives a bad result, so always check the sensor and its wiring before you suspect the computer."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"ECUs are very rarely the fault. Check the power, ground, sensor and actuator circuits first, and only suspect the ECU after you have ruled these out."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m08-l2", "title": "Sensors", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Sensors turn physical conditions into electrical signals. This lesson lists the main engine sensors, the sort of signal they send and what happens when they fail."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Temperature sensors"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The **coolant temperature sensor** and **intake air temperature sensor** are usually **thermistors**, resistors whose resistance changes with temperature. Most are **NTC** types, so their resistance falls as temperature rises. The ECU sends a 5 V reference through a fixed resistor and measures the voltage at the sensor. A failed coolant sensor can cause hard starting, rich running and a fan that never stops, because the ECU thinks the engine is cold."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Air measurement"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Mass airflow (MAF) sensor:** a heated element measures the air mass. A dirty or failed MAF gives a wrong fuel quantity.","**Manifold absolute pressure (MAP) sensor:** measures manifold pressure. A leak or failed sensor causes poor idle, hesitation and wrong fuel.","**Throttle position sensor (TPS):** reports the throttle angle, typically 0.5 V closed to about 4.5 V wide open. A faulty one causes poor idle, surging and hesitation."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Speed and position"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The **crankshaft position sensor** gives engine speed and piston position. The **camshaft position sensor** tells the ECU which stroke each cylinder is on. These can be **inductive** (generating an AC voltage), or **Hall-effect** (switching a signal between low and high). If the crankshaft sensor fails, the engine usually stops or will not start, because the ECU does not know when to fire the injectors and coils."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Knock and oxygen sensors"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **knock sensor** is a piezoelectric device that detects the vibration of knock, so the ECU can retard the timing. The **oxygen (lambda) sensor** in the exhaust measures how much oxygen is left in the exhaust gas. A conventional sensor switches between about 0.1 V (lean) and 0.9 V (rich) around lambda 1. A **wide-band** sensor measures the actual air-fuel ratio. Contaminated or aged oxygen sensors react slowly, causing poor fuel economy and emissions."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Sensor","Measures","Failed sensor causes"],"rows":[["Coolant temperature","Engine temperature","Hard start, rich mixture, fan fault"],["MAF","Air mass","Wrong fuel, hesitation, poor economy"],["MAP","Manifold pressure","Poor idle, hesitation"],["TPS","Throttle angle","Surge, hesitation, limp mode"],["Crankshaft position","Speed and position","No start, stalling"],["Oxygen","Exhaust oxygen","Wrong mixture, poor economy"]],"caption":"Main sensors and failure effects"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Mass airflow sensors in detail"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **hot-wire** or **hot-film** MAF sensor keeps a thin heated element at a fixed temperature above the air temperature. Air flowing past cools it, and the circuit supplies more current to hold the temperature. The current, converted to a voltage or frequency, shows the mass of air. Because it reads mass directly, it needs no correction for air temperature and height. Dirt, oil mist from a leaking breather, or a poor air filter coats the element and makes it read low, so the engine runs lean. Clean it with a special sensor cleaner, never with a brush or compressed air."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Is the MAF reading sensible?","steps":["A 2.0 litre four-stroke engine takes in its swept volume once every two turns. At 800 rpm, the swept air is 2.0 × 800 ÷ 120 = 13.3 litres per second.","At idle the throttle is almost closed, so the cylinders fill only to about 30% of their volume. Air is 13.3 × 0.30 = 4.0 litres per second.","Air has a density of about 1.2 g per litre, so the mass is 4.0 × 1.2 = 4.8 g/s. A MAF reading of about 3 to 6 g/s at idle is therefore sensible.","At 3,000 rpm with the throttle wide open and 90% filling, the airflow is 2.0 × 3,000 ÷ 120 × 0.90 × 1.2 = 54 g/s. A reading far below this at full throttle suggests a dirty sensor or a restricted intake."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Pressure sensors"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **MAP sensor** has a small silicon diaphragm that changes its resistance as the pressure on it changes. With the ignition on and the engine off, it should read atmospheric pressure, about 100 kPa at sea level, less at height. At idle the reading falls to about 30 to 45 kPa. On a turbocharged engine the value rises above atmospheric when boost builds. Test with a hand vacuum pump and a meter. A reading that does not change, or that stays at the atmospheric value at idle, means a blocked or disconnected hose or a faulty sensor."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Other useful sensors"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Accelerator pedal position sensor:** usually two tracks, with opposite or different signals, so that the ECU can check them against each other.","**Wheel speed sensors:** give vehicle speed to the engine and gearbox control and also to the ABS.","**Fuel level, pressure and temperature sensors:** report to the ECU for fuel metering and warnings.","**Brake switch and clutch switch:** tell the ECU that the driver is braking or has pressed the clutch. A faulty brake switch can cause strange engine faults, because the ECU sees conflicting signals."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Know what normal looks like. A coolant sensor should read the same as an actual thermometer, and a TPS should rise smoothly as the throttle opens with no jumps."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m08-l3", "title": "Sensor circuits, signals and testing", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A sensor is only useful if the ECU can read its signal correctly. Understanding the circuit lets you test a sensor and its wiring with a meter or scope, and tell a faulty sensor from a faulty wire. This lesson explains the main kinds of signal and how to check them."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The three-wire sensor"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Many sensors, such as the throttle position sensor, MAP sensor and accelerator pedal sensor, have three wires: a **5 V reference** supply from the ECU, a **signal** back to the ECU and a **sensor earth** return. The sensor changes the voltage on the signal wire in proportion to what it measures. Typical values are 0.5 V at one end of the range and 4.5 V at the other. A signal outside this band, for example below 0.2 V or above 4.8 V, points to an open circuit or a short and sets a code."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Fault","Result on the signal wire"],"rows":[["Reference wire open","Signal falls to about 0 V"],["Signal wire open","Signal at 0 V or 5 V, depending on the ECU, and does not respond to changes"],["Signal wire shorted to earth","Signal at 0 V"],["Signal wire shorted to the reference or to battery","Signal at 5 V or higher"],["Earth wire open","Signal rises above normal, often to 5 V"]],"caption":"Check the three wires at the sensor connector with the key on, then check the signal while the sensor is working."},
      {"t":"p","text":"Several sensors often share one 5 V reference. A short on the wire of one sensor can pull the reference down and cause several sensors to fail together. If many codes appear at once for unrelated sensors, think of a shared reference or earth."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Types of sensor signal"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Type","How it works","Examples","Typical signal"],"rows":[["Variable resistor","Resistance changes with temperature or position","Coolant and air temperature (NTC), throttle position and pedal sensors","A voltage between about 0.5 and 4.5 V"],["Voltage generator","Produces its own voltage","Inductive crankshaft sensor, oxygen sensor, knock sensor","AC or small DC voltage"],["Hall effect or digital","Switches a signal on and off","Cam and crank sensors, wheel speed sensors","Square wave between about 0 and 5 V or 12 V"],["Pressure sensor","A flexing plate changes a resistor value","MAP sensor, fuel and oil pressure sensors, boost sensor","About 0.5 to 4.5 V, rising with pressure"],["Frequency output","The frequency changes with the measurement","Some MAF sensors","A square wave whose frequency rises with air flow"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Reading a crankshaft signal"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A crankshaft position sensor reads a toothed wheel on the crankshaft. Most wheels have a number of teeth with one or two missing. A **36-1** wheel has 36 positions with one tooth missing, and a **60-2** wheel has 60 positions with two missing. The gap gives the ECU a reference for the position of a known cylinder, and the teeth give the speed and angle. On a scope, an inductive sensor makes an AC pattern that grows in height with engine speed and has a long gap at the missing tooth. A Hall sensor gives a square wave. A damaged tooth, a cracked wheel, a gap that is too large or metal debris on a magnetic sensor causes misfires, stalling and no start."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Checking the speed from the teeth","steps":["A 60-2 wheel has 58 real teeth. At idle the ECU sees 58 pulses per revolution.","At 800 rpm the sensor sends 800 × 58 ÷ 60 = 773 pulses per second, a frequency of about 773 Hz.","At 6,000 rpm the frequency is 6,000 × 58 ÷ 60 = 5,800 Hz. A scope must be set to sample fast enough to show it. If your tool cannot, you will see a false pattern."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Testing a sensor in practice"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Read the live data on the scan tool and compare it with what you expect. For example, a cold engine should show the same temperature as the air temperature.","Check the sensor connector for damage, corrosion and bent pins, and wiggle the wires to look for an intermittent fault.","Measure the reference voltage and the earth at the connector with the key on. A good reference is 5 V. A good earth has less than about 0.1 V.","Measure the signal and see that it changes smoothly when the sensor is operated, for example the throttle opened slowly. A sudden jump or a dead spot means a worn track.","For a resistance sensor, measure the resistance with the connector off and compare it with the manual table at the same temperature.","If the sensor and its wiring are good, check the ECU connection and the earth."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Always test the wiring first. Most sensor faults are caused by corroded connectors, broken wires and poor earths, not by the sensor itself."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Back-probe a connector with a thin pin or probe, so you do not damage the terminal. Never push a meter probe into the front of a connector."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m08-l4", "title": "Actuators and control", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The ECU switches actuators to control the engine. This lesson covers the main ones and a simple way of testing them."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Injectors and ignition coils"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The ECU opens each injector by switching its ground (or power) for a period of time. It switches the coil primary current off at the moment of ignition. These switches are called **drivers** and sit inside the ECU. A short circuit in an actuator can damage the driver, so check the resistance of a suspect part before you connect it."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Idle and throttle control"},
      {"t":"p","text":"On older vehicles an **idle air control valve** lets a small amount of air bypass the throttle. On modern vehicles a **throttle motor** (drive-by-wire) moves the throttle plate to the ECU's request, with feedback from two throttle position sensors."},
      {"t":"h","text":"How the ECU drives an actuator"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Many actuators are driven by **pulse width modulation** (PWM). The ECU switches the supply on and off many times a second, and the share of the time that it is on, the **duty cycle**, sets the average power. A solenoid valve driven at 75% duty is open more than one driven at 25%. A scan tool shows the duty cycle as a percentage. A **stepper motor**, as used in some idle controllers, is moved a set number of steps by four coils switched in sequence, so the ECU always knows its position, and it recalibrates against a stop when the engine is switched off."},
      {"t":"h","text":"EGR and purge valves"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**EGR valve:** lets some exhaust gas return to the intake, reducing combustion temperature and nitrogen oxide formation.","**Purge valve:** lets fuel vapour from the charcoal canister into the engine to be burned.","**Variable timing solenoid:** controls oil to the valve timing mechanism."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Relays and drivers"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The ECU often drives relays for the fuel pump, fan and other high-current loads. Relays are explained in module 9. A relay lets a small current from the ECU control a large current for the load."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Testing an actuator"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Check the power and ground at the connector with the key on.","Measure the resistance of the actuator and compare it with the manual.","Use a scan tool with a bidirectional test (actuator test) to command the part on and off.","Listen or feel for operation, or use an oscilloscope or test lamp on the signal wire."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Relays and solenoid control"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The ECU drives high-current parts such as the fuel pump, cooling fan and air conditioning clutch through relays, so that its own drivers carry only a small current. A relay can fail in the coil, the contacts or the connector. If the ECU commands a part on and nothing happens, check the relay with the scan tool or by swapping with an identical one. A **variable valve timing solenoid** (oil control valve) controls oil flow to the cam phaser. Test its resistance and the signal, and remove it to check that its screen and passages are clean. Dirty oil and low oil level cause faults here."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Actuator","What the ECU controls","A test"],"rows":[["Fuel pump relay","On for a moment at key on, then while the engine runs","Listen for the prime, check for 12 V at the pump"],["Cooling fan relay","On above a set coolant temperature","Command it on with the scan tool and watch the fan"],["EGR valve","Opens at part load when warm","Command it open at idle and see if the idle roughens"],["Purge valve","Opens at warm, light-load conditions","Listen for the click, check for vacuum when commanded"],["VVT solenoid","Moves the cam timing with oil pressure","Command it with the scan tool and watch the cam angle"]]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"A test lamp with a low-power bulb is safer than a probe on ECU outputs. Never use a normal bulb that draws a large current on an ECU circuit."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never short an ECU output wire to ground or power to see whether a part works. You can destroy the driver circuit in the ECU."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m08-l5", "title": "Oxygen sensors and closed-loop fuel control", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The oxygen sensor is the key to the closed loop that keeps the mixture at lambda 1 and the catalytic converter working. It is also one of the most common parts to age and cause emission faults. This lesson explains how the different types work, how to test them and what their signals tell you."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The narrow-band (zirconia) sensor"},
      {"t":"p","text":"This sensor has a ceramic element made of zirconium dioxide. When it is hot, over about 350 °C, it generates a voltage that depends on the difference between the oxygen in the exhaust and the oxygen in the outside air. With a rich exhaust it makes about 0.8 to 0.9 V, and with a lean exhaust about 0.1 V, with a very quick switch at lambda 1. The ECU therefore knows only whether the mixture is richer or leaner than 14.7:1, not by how much. It adjusts the fuel in small steps, so the voltage swings from high to low about once or twice a second at idle. The sensor has a **heater** so that it gets hot quickly, and the heater circuit has its own code."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Signal on a scope","What it tells you"],"rows":[["Switches between about 0.2 and 0.8 V, steady, one or two times a second","A healthy sensor in closed loop"],["Flat at about 0.45 V","Open loop, or a dead sensor"],["Stuck high (0.8 to 0.9 V)","Rich mixture or sensor fault"],["Stuck low (below 0.2 V)","Lean mixture, an air leak, or a dead sensor"],["Slow switching, a rounded wave","An aged or contaminated sensor"],["Fast, small and ragged","A misfire or a bad leak"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Wide-band sensors"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **wide-band** (air-fuel ratio) sensor has two cells, a sensing cell like a zirconia sensor and a pump cell. The ECU drives a current through the pump cell to keep the sensing cell at its balance point, and the amount of current needed gives a precise reading of the mixture over a wide range, from very rich to very lean. It is needed on engines that run lean for economy and on diesels. A scan tool shows the result as lambda or the air-fuel ratio. Wide-band sensors need a heater and a reference circuit. Test them with a scan tool or the manual's procedure. Do not use a plain ohmmeter on the sensor wires or you can damage the pump cell."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Sensor positions"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["The **upstream** sensor sits before the catalytic converter. It controls the fuel.","The **downstream** sensor sits after the converter. It monitors the converter. If the catalyst works, the downstream sensor gives a steady, nearly flat signal, because the converter stores and releases oxygen. If its signal mirrors the upstream sensor, the converter is not working, and a catalyst code sets.","Engines with two cylinder banks, such as a V6, have a sensor on each bank. **Bank 1** is the bank containing cylinder 1."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Faults and testing"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Fault","Common cause"],"rows":[["Slow response","Age, silicone or lead contamination, or an engine burning oil or coolant"],["Heater circuit code","A failed heater element, a blown fuse or a wiring fault"],["Sensor stuck lean","Air leak in the exhaust before the sensor, a failed sensor or a lean misfire"],["Sensor stuck rich","Rich mixture from a leaking injector or high fuel pressure, or a contaminated sensor"]]},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Check the heater resistance with the connector off, using an ohmmeter, and compare with the manual. It is usually a few ohms.","Check the sensor wiring for damage from heat, and look for exhaust leaks upstream of the sensor, which let air in and give a false lean reading.","Bring the engine to working temperature and watch the sensor on a scope or scan tool at idle and at 2,500 rpm. Look for fast, regular switching.","Snap the throttle open quickly. The sensor should go rich and then lean on closing quickly. A slow response means an aged sensor."]},
      {"t":"example","title":"Reading short-term trim with the sensor","steps":["At idle, a warm engine shows the upstream voltage switching around 0.45 V and short-term trim swinging a few per cent either side of zero. This is normal.","If the sensor reads stuck low and the trim climbs to +25%, the ECU is adding fuel to try to correct what it sees as a lean condition. If there is in fact no lean condition, the sensor is faulty, and the result is a rich engine with black exhaust and fouled plugs.","Confirm with a gas analyser before replacing parts. If the real exhaust is rich, the sensor is lying, so replace it. If the exhaust is lean, the sensor is right and you should look for the leak."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"The oxygen sensor reports the result of everything else. If it reads lean or rich, find out whether the mixture really is lean or rich, and why, before you replace it."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Exhaust parts are very hot. Let the system cool before you remove a sensor. Use the right sensor socket, and apply anti-seize only if the maker allows, and only on the threads, never on the tip."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m08-l6", "title": "Emission formation and control", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Burning fuel produces pollutants as well as power. Emission systems reduce them so vehicles pollute less. This lesson explains what is produced and how it is controlled."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The main pollutants"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Pollutant","What it is","Main cause"],"rows":[["Carbon monoxide (CO)","Poisonous gas from incomplete burning","Rich mixture"],["Hydrocarbons (HC)","Unburnt fuel","Misfire, rich mixture, cold engine"],["Oxides of nitrogen (NOx)","Harmful gases that form in hot combustion","High combustion temperature, lean mixture"],["Particulates","Fine soot particles","Diesel combustion, rich or poor burning"]],"caption":"Pollutants and their causes"},
      {"t":"p","text":"These pollutants matter because of what they do. Carbon monoxide poisons by displacing oxygen in the blood. Hydrocarbons and nitrogen oxides react in sunlight to form smog, and NOx also forms acid rain and affects breathing. Particulates enter the lungs and are linked to heart and lung disease. Carbon dioxide (CO2) is not a poison, but it is a greenhouse gas that is produced in proportion to the fuel burned, so the best way to reduce it is to burn less fuel."},
      {"t":"example","title":"How much CO2 does an engine make?","steps":["Burning 1 litre of petrol produces about 2.3 kg of CO2.","A car that uses 8 litres per 100 km therefore emits 8 × 2.3 = 18.4 kg of CO2 per 100 km, or 184 g/km.","Diesel gives about 2.7 kg of CO2 per litre, so a diesel that uses 6 L/100 km emits 6 × 2.7 = 16.2 kg per 100 km, or 162 g/km."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Catalytic converter"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **three-way catalytic converter** in the exhaust uses a coating of precious metals on a ceramic honeycomb. It converts CO and HC to carbon dioxide and water, and NOx back to nitrogen. It works best near lambda 1, when the exhaust has a balance of oxidising and reducing gases. The oxygen sensor before the converter lets the ECU hold the mixture there."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"catalytic-converter"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Warm-up and light-off"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A catalytic converter only works well when it is hot, usually above about 300 °C. This is called **light-off**. Most pollution from a vehicle is made in the first minutes after a cold start, before the converter and the oxygen sensor are working. Short trips are therefore bad for emissions. Many engines use heaters in the oxygen sensor and a converter close to the engine to shorten the warm-up time."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Other emission systems"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**EGR:** recirculates some exhaust to lower combustion temperature and cut NOx.","**Evaporative emission system (EVAP):** traps fuel vapour from the tank in a charcoal canister, then burns it in the engine.","**PCV:** returns crankcase gases to the intake (module 5).","**Diesel particulate filter (DPF):** traps soot and burns it off periodically in a process called regeneration.","**SCR (selective catalytic reduction):** injects a urea solution, called **AdBlue** or diesel exhaust fluid, into the exhaust to turn NOx into nitrogen and water."]},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"emission-controls"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Emission standards"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Governments set limits for the pollutants a vehicle may emit, and the limits become stricter in steps. The European standards are called Euro 1 to Euro 6, and in the United States there are federal and state standards. A vehicle is built to the standard in force when it is made, and an imported used vehicle may have been built for a different market than the one where it is registered. This matters because a more advanced emission system, such as a diesel particulate filter or SCR, needs low-sulphur fuel and special fluids to work. On a vehicle that is run on poor fuel, those parts can fail or be damaged quickly."},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Never use leaded petrol in a vehicle with a catalytic converter. Lead coats the catalyst and destroys it permanently.","Use fuel with low sulphur wherever possible. Sulphur reduces the life of catalytic converters and oxygen sensors.","If the vehicle comes from another market, find out which standard it was built to and which fluids it needs."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Every emission device has a job and the engine is calibrated to work with it. Removing or bypassing one changes the way the engine runs and adds to pollution."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Use only the fluid and filter type specified for the DPF and SCR systems, and never use the wrong additive in the fuel. Wrong products can block the filter or damage the catalyst."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m08-l7", "title": "EGR, EVAP, catalyst and diesel exhaust treatment", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Besides the three-way catalyst, vehicles have several systems that cut particular emissions. This lesson explains how each works, how it fails and how to test it. Understanding them also shows why removing a part never solves a fault."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Exhaust gas recirculation (EGR)"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Part of the exhaust gas is fed back into the intake. It replaces some of the air with a gas that does not burn, so the peak combustion temperature falls, and nitrogen oxides, which form at high temperature, fall with it. The ECU opens the valve at part load and warm temperatures, and shuts it at idle and full power. Older valves were opened by vacuum, and modern ones by an electric motor with a position sensor. Many diesels also have an **EGR cooler** that cools the gas first."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Fault","Symptoms"],"rows":[["Valve stuck open or leaking","Rough idle, stalling, hesitation, poor power"],["Valve stuck closed or blocked passages","Knock or pinging, higher NOx, an insufficient flow code"],["Cooler leaking (diesel)","Coolant loss and white smoke"],["Carbon build-up","Sticking, sluggish valve, partial blockage"]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"To test an EGR valve, use the scan tool to command it open at idle. A working valve makes the idle rough or stall. If the idle is unchanged, the valve or the passage is blocked. Clean carbon from the valve and passages with the correct cleaner, and replace a valve that does not move."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Evaporative emission control (EVAP)"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Petrol evaporates from the tank, even when the engine is off. Rather than let the vapour escape, the **EVAP system** captures it. Vapour flows from the tank to a **charcoal canister**, which absorbs it. When the engine is warm, the ECU opens a **purge valve**, and fresh air drawn through the canister carries the vapour into the intake to burn. A **vent valve** on the canister opens it to the air, and closes it during leak tests. A **pressure sensor** in the tank or lines lets the ECU test for leaks."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Code or symptom","Likely cause"],"rows":[["Large leak code (P0455)","Loose or missing fuel cap, a disconnected hose"],["Small leak code (P0442, P0456)","Tiny leak in a hose, valve, canister or tank seal"],["Rough idle after refuelling","A stuck-open purge valve that pulls in vapour"],["Hard to refuel, the nozzle clicks off","A blocked vent valve or canister"],["A strong fuel smell","A leak or a saturated canister"]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"First check the fuel cap, its seal and the filler neck. If they are good, use a smoke machine with a low-pressure supply to find leaks, as the manual says. Never pressurise the fuel tank with compressed air."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Secondary air injection"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Some engines blow fresh air into the exhaust just after a cold start. The air helps burn the unburnt fuel and heats the catalyst so that it lights off sooner. A pump and valves do this for the first minute. Faults give a code, noise and a long time to reach closed loop."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Catalyst and particulate filter"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The three-way catalyst is covered earlier. In a diesel, the **diesel oxidation catalyst** burns carbon monoxide and hydrocarbons. The **diesel particulate filter** (DPF) is a ceramic honeycomb with channels blocked alternately so that the gas passes through the walls, leaving the soot behind. When the filter gets full of soot, it has to be cleaned by **regeneration**, burning the soot at over 550 °C."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Regeneration type","How it happens"],"rows":[["Passive","Long, hot drives, such as on a motorway, raise the exhaust temperature to burn the soot slowly"],["Active","The ECU injects extra fuel late in the cycle to heat the filter. The driver may notice a changed idle, a fan running and a slight smell."],["Forced (service)","A technician starts a regeneration with a scan tool when the filter is too full for the vehicle to do it itself"]]},
      {"t":"p","text":"Short journeys do not give the filter enough heat. A warning light comes on when the filter is loading, and the driver should take a longer drive at speed as the manual says. If it is ignored, the filter blocks, the engine loses power and it may need to be cleaned or replaced. Fuel dilution of the oil from repeated incomplete regenerations is another risk, so check the oil level, which can rise."},
      {"t":"h","text":"SCR and AdBlue"},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Selective catalytic reduction** reduces nitrogen oxides in the exhaust of many diesels. A fluid called **diesel exhaust fluid** (a 32.5% urea solution, sold as AdBlue and under other names) is injected before a special catalyst, where it changes to ammonia and converts NOx to nitrogen and water. A tank holds the fluid, a pump and injector deliver it, and **NOx sensors** before and after check the result. The tank has a heater because the fluid freezes at about −11 °C. Use only the correct fluid, never water, and never add anything else to the tank. If the fluid runs out, the engine will refuse to start after a set distance."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Each emission device has a job, and a fault means something upstream is wrong. A blocked filter or a failed converter is usually the result of a misfire, an oil leak or a bad fuel mixture. Fix the cause or the new part will fail too."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"A catalyst, a DPF and the exhaust get hot enough to ignite dry grass and clothing. Do not park a running or recently run vehicle over dry vegetation."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m08-l8", "title": "Exhaust gas analysis", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"An exhaust gas analyser measures what comes out of the tailpipe, and tells you about combustion, mixture and the catalyst in a way that no other tool can. It is used for emission testing, and as a diagnostic tool. This lesson explains what each gas means and how to read the results."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The gases and what they tell you"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Gas","Unit","What it shows","Healthy engine with a working catalyst (warm, idle)"],"rows":[["Carbon monoxide (CO)","% by volume","Rich mixture and incomplete burning","Less than about 0.5%"],["Hydrocarbons (HC)","ppm (parts per million)","Unburnt fuel, from a misfire, a rich mixture or poor sealing","Less than about 100 ppm"],["Carbon dioxide (CO2)","% by volume","How complete the burning is. Highest near lambda 1.","About 13.5 to 15.5%"],["Oxygen (O2)","% by volume","Leftover oxygen. High when lean or with a misfire.","Less than about 0.5%"],["Nitrogen oxides (NOx)","ppm","Hot, lean combustion","Low, checked with a five-gas machine at load"]],"caption":"These are typical values. The limits for a legal test are set by the local regulation."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Reading the pattern"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Reading","Likely cause"],"rows":[["High CO, high HC, low O2, low CO2","Rich mixture"],["Low CO, high O2, HC high at extremes","Lean mixture, and a lean misfire if the lean limit is exceeded"],["High HC and high O2, CO low","A misfire, as unburnt air and fuel both pass through"],["High CO and HC, O2 normal","Catalytic converter not working, or very late ignition"],["Normal CO2 but high CO after the catalyst, falls on a pre-catalyst probe","Catalyst failure"],["CO2 low and O2 high","An exhaust leak ahead of the probe, which dilutes the sample"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Lambda and the analyser"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Many analysers calculate **lambda** from the four gases. A reading between 0.97 and 1.03 is within the normal range for a catalytic converter engine. The analyser gives a good independent check of the oxygen sensor, since it is a separate instrument. If lambda from the analyser is off while the ECU thinks it is correct, the oxygen sensor or the fuel trim is wrong."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Taking a good reading"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Warm up the engine and the converter fully. A cold engine gives high readings.","Warm up and zero the analyser, and check the filters and the water trap.","Check the exhaust for leaks, since a leak lets air into the sample.","Insert the probe at least 30 cm into the tailpipe, or as far as the maker says. Do not let it touch the sides.","Let the readings settle for a few moments at idle, and then at about 2,500 rpm. Write them down.","Remove the probe after the engine has stopped, or remove it carefully, and let it cool."]},
      {"t":"example","title":"Diagnosing a failed test","steps":["A car fails with CO 2.8% and HC 450 ppm, but O2 is 0.2%. This pattern points to a rich mixture, because both CO and HC are high and oxygen is almost nil.","The scan tool shows the long-term fuel trim at −18%, so the ECU is already removing fuel. The ECU is correcting for an extra source of fuel.","The fuel pressure holds when the engine is off, so injectors are probably not leaking. The purge valve is commanded off but fuel vapour is drawn into the engine. A stuck-open purge valve is found. After replacement CO falls to 0.3%."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Diesel smoke tests"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A diesel is tested with an **opacity meter**. A probe takes a sample of the exhaust and measures how much light the smoke blocks. The result is a **k value** or a percentage opacity. The test is done by quickly pressing the accelerator to the floor several times to show the maximum smoke. The limit depends on the local regulation. Heavy black smoke points to too much fuel for the air, a worn injector, a blocked air filter or a boost leak, and blue smoke to burning oil."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A gas analyser lets you see inside combustion. Always check the readings together, as one number alone can mislead."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Use the analyser in a ventilated place. Exhaust contains carbon monoxide, and the hot tailpipe and probe can burn you."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m08-l9", "title": "Trouble codes and OBD", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"When the ECU detects a fault it stores a code and may turn on the warning lamp. Reading codes and data is the first step in most diagnosis, but codes need to be understood correctly."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The warning lamp"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The malfunction indicator lamp (MIL), often a picture of an engine, tells the driver that the system has detected a fault that may affect emissions. A flashing lamp usually means a serious misfire that can damage the catalytic converter. Stop driving and have it checked."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Code structure"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The generic **OBD-II** system gives codes of a letter and four digits, such as P0301."},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**First letter:** P is powertrain (engine and transmission), B is body, C is chassis, U is network communication.","**First digit:** 0 is a generic code, the same on all vehicles. 1 is a manufacturer-specific code.","**Third character:** the system, for example fuel and air metering, ignition and misfire, emission controls, idle control or the computer.","**Last two digits:** the specific fault."]},
      {"t":"table","head":["Code","Meaning"],"rows":[["P0300","Random or multiple cylinder misfire"],["P0301","Cylinder 1 misfire"],["P0171","System too lean (bank 1)"],["P0420","Catalyst efficiency below threshold (bank 1)"]],"caption":"Examples of generic codes"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Freeze-frame, live data and readiness"},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Freeze-frame data** is a snapshot of the sensor values at the moment the fault was stored. It tells you the engine speed, load and temperature when the fault happened. **Live data** shows the sensor values in real time. **Readiness monitors** are self-tests that the ECU must complete before the vehicle can pass an emissions test."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Clearing codes responsibly"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Clearing codes also clears the freeze-frame data and resets the readiness monitors. Write the codes and data down before you clear them. Clear them only when the fault has been repaired, and then check that the code does not return."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The diagnostic socket"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Pin","Use"],"rows":[["4","Chassis earth"],["5","Signal earth"],["16","Battery positive (always live)"],["6 and 14","CAN high and CAN low (most modern vehicles)"],["7","K-line (older protocols)"],["2 and 10","J1850 bus (some older vehicles)"]],"caption":"The standard 16-pin OBD-II socket is usually under the dashboard on the driver's side."},
      {"t":"p","text":"If a scan tool will not power up, check that pin 16 has battery voltage and pins 4 and 5 are connected to earth. A blown fuse for the cigarette lighter, which often supplies pin 16, is a common reason. If the tool powers up but will not communicate, check the vehicle's ignition is on and that the tool supports the vehicle."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The standard modes"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Mode","What it gives"],"rows":[["01","Current data from sensors (live data)"],["02","Freeze-frame data"],["03","Stored (confirmed) fault codes"],["04","Clears codes and resets monitors"],["06","Results of the on-board test monitors"],["07","Pending codes, seen once but not yet confirmed"],["09","Vehicle information such as the VIN"],["0A","Permanent codes that cannot be cleared until the fault is repaired and re-tested"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Common generic codes"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Code","Meaning","First checks"],"rows":[["P0016, P0017","Crankshaft and camshaft position do not match","Timing chain or belt, phaser, sensors"],["P0101","MAF sensor range or performance","Air leaks, dirty sensor, wiring"],["P0113","Intake air temperature sensor high","Open circuit, connector, sensor"],["P0117, P0118","Coolant temperature sensor low or high","Short or open circuit, sensor, connector"],["P0171, P0174","System lean, bank 1 or bank 2","Vacuum leak, fuel pressure, MAF sensor"],["P0172, P0175","System rich, bank 1 or bank 2","Injector leak, fuel pressure, purge valve"],["P0300 to P0308","Random misfire or a specific cylinder","Plugs, coils, injectors, compression"],["P0335","Crankshaft position sensor circuit","Sensor, wiring, reluctor wheel"],["P0420, P0430","Catalyst efficiency below threshold","Misfire, oxygen sensors, exhaust leaks, converter"],["P0440 to P0457","Evaporative system faults","Fuel cap, hoses, purge and vent valves"]]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A code points to a circuit or system, not to a part. A lean code, for example, may be caused by a vacuum leak, low fuel pressure or a dirty MAF sensor, and not an oxygen sensor fault."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Compare live data against what you expect. Coolant temperature should rise smoothly to about 90 °C, short-term fuel trims should swing around zero and long-term trim should stay close to zero."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m08-l10", "title": "Diagnosing with live data, freeze frame and monitors", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Reading a fault code takes ten seconds. Working out what it means takes skill. This lesson shows how to use live data, freeze-frame information and the readiness monitors to turn a code into a confirmed fault, with examples that use real numbers."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Start with the freeze frame"},
      {"t":"p","text":"When a code is stored, the ECU saves a snapshot: engine speed, load, coolant temperature, fuel trim, vehicle speed and other values. This tells you the conditions in which the fault happened. A lean code that set at 3,000 rpm and high load points to fuel delivery. One that set at idle and warm points to an air leak. A misfire stored with a cold engine may be a cold-start fault. Always write down the freeze frame before you clear anything."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Useful live data to watch"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Data item","Healthy engine (warm, idle)"],"rows":[["Engine speed","About 650 to 900 rpm, steady"],["Coolant temperature","Rises to about 85 to 105 °C and holds steady"],["Intake air temperature","Close to the air temperature, a little above"],["MAF reading","About 2 to 5 g/s at idle, rising smoothly with revs"],["MAP reading","About 25 to 45 kPa at idle, 100 kPa with the engine off"],["Throttle position","About 0 to 5% at idle, rising smoothly to 100%"],["Short-term fuel trim","Swinging a few per cent either side of zero"],["Long-term fuel trim","Within about ±5%"],["Upstream oxygen sensor","Switching between about 0.1 and 0.9 V"],["Ignition advance","About 5° to 15° before TDC at idle"],["Battery voltage","About 13.8 to 14.7 V with the engine running"]],"caption":"These are typical numbers. The manual or a known good vehicle gives the right figures."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The monitors"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The ECU runs self-tests, the **monitors**, on the catalyst, oxygen sensors, EGR, EVAP and misfire. Each test only runs when conditions are right, and the result is flagged as complete or not complete. A **drive cycle** is a pattern of driving that sets the conditions, such as a cold start, idle, steady cruising and a deceleration. The readiness status can only be seen as complete after a drive cycle, so a vehicle must have been driven properly after a code clear. A vehicle that arrives for a test with the codes just cleared will show incomplete monitors and may be refused a test."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Misfire counters"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The ECU detects a misfire by watching small changes in crankshaft speed. It counts misfires for each cylinder. A scan tool shows the count, so you can see which cylinder is at fault, and whether the misfire is at idle, under load, cold or hot. A random misfire across all cylinders points to something common, such as fuel, air or a weak spark supply. A misfire on one cylinder points to its plug, coil, injector or compression."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Worked examples"},
      {"t":"example","title":"Lean code with a clean air filter","steps":["The code is P0171. Freeze frame shows 800 rpm, warm engine, short-term trim +14%, long-term trim +21%.","At 2,500 rpm in the live data the long-term trim is +7%. The pattern shows a leak that is large at idle and small at speed.","Check the MAF value. At idle it is 3.1 g/s, which is normal for the engine, so the MAF is probably right. The leak is after the sensor.","A smoke machine shows smoke at a cracked brake booster hose. After replacing the hose both trims return to near zero, and the code is cleared with the monitors drive-cycled."]},
      {"t":"example","title":"A misfire on cylinder 2 only","steps":["The code is P0302. The misfire counter shows cylinder 2 only, mainly under load.","Swapping the coil with cylinder 3 moves the code to P0303, so the coil is faulty. A new coil is fitted.","Because the old plug was worn, the other plugs are checked and replaced as a set, and the plug gaps are set. The misfire count falls to zero on a road test."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Do not chase a code. Read the freeze frame, look at live data, and form a theory. Test the theory with a measurement, and only then replace parts."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Record the live data on a drive with a data logger, and review it when you are not driving. Intermittent faults show up as a sudden change at a particular moment."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m08-l11", "title": "Emission and management faults", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"This lesson covers common faults in emission and engine management systems, and how to prepare a vehicle for an emissions test."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Fuel trims as a diagnostic"},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Short-term fuel trim** is the quick correction the ECU makes second by second. **Long-term fuel trim** is the learned correction stored for the longer term. A positive trim means the ECU is adding fuel. Trims above about plus 10 to 15% suggest a lean condition such as a vacuum leak, a dirty MAF sensor or low fuel pressure. Large negative trims suggest too much fuel, such as a leaking injector or purge valve."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Observation","Likely cause"],"rows":[["Lean at idle, normal at higher speed","Vacuum leak"],["Lean at higher load and speed","Low fuel pressure or a dirty MAF"],["Rich at all speeds","Leaking injector, high fuel pressure, stuck purge valve"],["Oxygen sensor slow to switch","Aged or contaminated sensor"]],"caption":"Reading fuel trims"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Failed catalyst"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A catalyst can fail from age, contamination (oil, coolant or lead), or overheating. Signs include a rotten-egg smell, poor power, a very hot converter and a catalyst efficiency code. A blocked converter restricts the exhaust and can make the engine lose power at speed."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Misfire and converter damage"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A misfire sends raw fuel into the exhaust, where it burns in the converter and can melt it. A flashing MIL means a severe misfire. Repair misfires quickly."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Removing emission parts"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Removing a catalytic converter, DPF, EGR valve or oxygen sensor is illegal in most countries. It increases the pollution from the vehicle, can make the vehicle fail an inspection, and may not even cure the fault. Do not carry out illegal modifications. Repair or replace the part properly."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Preparing for an emissions test"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Check there are no warning lights or stored codes.","Make sure the engine is warm and has run for enough time for the readiness monitors to complete.","Replace dirty oil and filters, and fix all exhaust leaks and misfires.","Check that the air filter, plugs and fuel system are in good order."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Diagnostic examples"},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Example 1: P0171 (system too lean).** A car has a hesitating idle and a lean code. At idle the long-term trim is +20%, and at 2,500 rpm it is +4%. The mass airflow reading is normal. A smoke test finds a split in the crankcase breather hose, a large vacuum leak. After replacement the trim falls to ±3% and the code does not return."},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Example 2: P0420 (catalyst efficiency).** A car has a catalyst efficiency code, but also a misfire code that was cleared a few weeks earlier. The rear oxygen sensor waveform mirrors the front one, so the converter is no longer storing oxygen. The converter is overheated. The cause, a worn coil that was misfiring, is repaired first, and then a new converter is fitted. Fitting the converter alone would have led to another failure."},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Example 3: Fails the emission test with high HC and CO.** A gas analyser shows CO at 3.5% and HC at 800 ppm with a normal oxygen level. This is a rich condition. A scan tool shows a coolant temperature stuck at −40 °C because the sensor connector has come off. The ECU thinks the engine is always freezing cold and adds extra fuel. Reconnecting the sensor corrects the mixture and the car passes."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"A catalytic converter can reach very high temperatures. Do not park a running vehicle over dry grass or leave it near fuel, and let the exhaust cool before you work on it."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A fault found by the scan tool is the start of diagnosis. Always confirm with a test before you replace a part."}
    ]}
  ],
  "practical": {"title":"Read codes and live data with a scan tool","objective":"Connect a scan tool to a training vehicle, read trouble codes, freeze-frame data and live data, and interpret the sensor values against a known-good range.","time":"75 minutes","tools":["OBD-II scan tool or reader with live data","A training vehicle with a working engine","Multimeter","Workshop manual or data sheet","Notepad and results table"],"safety":["Park the vehicle on level ground with the handbrake on and the gear in neutral or park.","Run the engine only where the exhaust is extracted outside the building.","Keep hands, hair and leads away from belts and fans while the engine runs.","Do not clear any codes until you have recorded them.","Do not disconnect any sensors with the engine running unless the instructor allows it."],"steps":["Find the diagnostic connector, usually under the dashboard, and plug in the scan tool.","Switch the ignition on and enter the vehicle details, or let the tool read the VIN.","Read and record any stored and pending trouble codes.","Read the freeze-frame data for each stored code and record it.","Start the engine and open the live data list.","Record the coolant temperature, short-term and long-term fuel trims and oxygen sensor readings at idle.","Raise the engine speed to about 2,500 rpm and record the same values again.","Compare each reading with the expected range from the manual.","Write down which readings are normal and which are not.","Check the readiness monitors and note any that are not complete.","Write a short note explaining what the codes and the data tell you about the engine.","Switch off and unplug the scan tool."],"checks":["I recorded all codes and freeze-frame data before doing anything else.","I read live data at idle and at higher speed and compared it with a known range.","I explained a code as pointing to a circuit or system and not just one part.","I did not clear codes before recording them.","I wrote a clear summary of the findings."]},
  "quiz": [
    {"id":"m08-q01","q":"What are the three main steps in the ECU's work?","options":["Charging the battery, storing fuel and igniting the mixture","Measuring the speed, the load and the temperature only","Inputs from sensors, processing, and outputs to actuators","Starting the engine, running the engine and stopping it"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m08-l1","explain":"Sensors feed the ECU, which processes the data and drives the actuators."},
    {"id":"m08-q02","q":"What is closed-loop operation?","options":["The ECU uses the oxygen sensor signal to correct the mixture continuously","The ECU uses stored values only and ignores the oxygen sensor completely","The engine runs with the throttle fully closed whenever it is idling","The fuel system is sealed so that no vapour can escape from the tank"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m08-l1","explain":"Closed loop uses feedback from the oxygen sensor. Open loop uses stored values, typically on a cold engine."},
    {"id":"m08-q03","q":"What is limp mode?","options":["A mode that the driver can select to save fuel on a long journey","A fail-safe condition where the ECU limits power after detecting a serious fault","A mode that switches the engine off after ten minutes of idling","A mode where the engine runs on only one cylinder bank to save fuel on the road"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m08-l1","explain":"The ECU limits power to protect the engine and the driver. A code is stored."},
    {"id":"m08-q04","q":"What does non-volatile memory in an ECU store?","options":["Fault codes and learned values","The fuel in the tank and the oil level","The tyre pressures and the wheel speeds","The radio stations and the clock settings"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m08-l1","explain":"Non-volatile memory keeps its contents when the power is off. It holds fault codes, adaptations and other learned data."},
    {"id":"m08-q05","q":"Why must the battery supply stay steady during an ECU software update?","options":["The update only runs in cold weather","A power loss can corrupt the software","A high voltage speeds up the update","The update erases the battery history"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m08-l1","explain":"If power is lost partway through writing the program, the ECU can be left unusable and may need workshop repair or replacement."},
    {"id":"m08-q06","q":"What happens if the coolant temperature sensor reports that the engine is always cold?","options":["The ECU enriches the mixture and the fan may run continuously","The engine will not turn over at all on any morning of the week","The ECU switches off the ignition coil permanently until the sensor is fixed","The ECU leans out the mixture to save fuel and the fan stays switched off"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m08-l2","explain":"The ECU thinks the engine is cold and adds fuel. A fan may run because of the high reading."},
    {"id":"m08-q07","q":"What does the MAF sensor measure?","options":["The mass of air entering the engine","The mass of fuel that is held in the fuel rail at that moment","The pressure of the exhaust gas as it leaves the cylinders","The speed at which the crankshaft is turning inside the block"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m08-l2","explain":"The ECU uses it to work out how much fuel to inject."},
    {"id":"m08-q08","q":"A throttle position sensor typically gives what voltage range?","options":["About 12 V when it is closed and about 0 V when it is fully open","About 0.5 V when closed and about 4.5 V when fully open","About 100 V when the throttle is held at wide open position","About 0 V at every throttle position, however far it is opened"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m08-l2","explain":"A smooth rise is the signal. Jumps or dropouts indicate a worn track."},
    {"id":"m08-q09","q":"Which sensor tells the ECU the engine speed and piston position?","options":["The coolant temperature sensor that sits in the thermostat housing","The oxygen sensor that is fitted in the exhaust manifold","The knock sensor, which is bolted to the side of the engine block","The crankshaft position sensor"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m08-l2","explain":"If it fails, the ECU cannot time the injectors and coils, so the engine usually will not run."},
    {"id":"m08-q10","q":"What does a conventional oxygen sensor measure?","options":["How much oxygen is in the exhaust gas, switching around lambda 1","The amount of fuel that is still in the exhaust pipe after burning","The temperature of the catalytic converter, reported in degrees Celsius","The pressure that is found in the intake manifold at idle"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m08-l2","explain":"The ECU uses this signal to keep the mixture at the ideal ratio."},
    {"id":"m08-q11","q":"A knock sensor works by doing what?","options":["Measuring the temperature of the cylinder head and the coolant","Counting the teeth on the flywheel as the crankshaft turns round","Detecting engine vibration so the ECU can retard the timing","Measuring the mass of air that passes the throttle and sending it on"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m08-l2","explain":"It is a piezoelectric sensor that senses the vibration pattern of knock."},
    {"id":"m08-q12","q":"What are the three wires of a typical throttle position sensor?","options":["A fuel feed, a spark lead and an oil return","A battery feed, a switch and a horn wire","A 5 V reference, a signal and an earth","A coolant sensor, a relay and a fuse line"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m08-l3","explain":"The ECU supplies 5 V, the sensor returns a signal voltage proportional to the angle, and the earth completes the circuit."},
    {"id":"m08-q13","q":"A sensor signal reads 0 V and does not change. Which fault could cause this?","options":["A dirty air filter in the airbox","A weak spark plug on one cylinder","Low washer fluid in the bottle","A signal wire shorted to earth"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m08-l3","explain":"0 V means the signal is held at earth or there is no supply. Check the reference at the connector and test for a short."},
    {"id":"m08-q14","q":"Several unrelated sensors set codes at once. What should you suspect?","options":["Seven sensors have failed at the same time","A fault on a shared reference or earth","The engine oil is the wrong grade","The tyres have been over-inflated"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m08-l3","explain":"Sensors often share one reference and earth. A fault on one wire can pull the supply down for them all."},
    {"id":"m08-q15","q":"A 60-2 crank wheel has how many real teeth?","options":["60","62","58","2"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m08-l3","explain":"The wheel has 60 positions with two teeth missing, so it has 58 real teeth. The gap gives the ECU a reference position."},
    {"id":"m08-q16","q":"At 3,000 rpm, how many pulses per second does a sensor on a 60-2 wheel give?","options":["About 50 pulses per second","About 290 pulses per second","About 5,800 pulses per second","About 2,900 pulses per second"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m08-l3","explain":"Pulses per second = rpm × 58 ÷ 60 = 3,000 × 58 ÷ 60 = 2,900. A scope must sample faster than this to show the wave."},
    {"id":"m08-q17","q":"What is the job of an EGR valve?","options":["To stop the exhaust gas from leaving the engine in cold weather","To release extra air into the exhaust so that it burns off any unburnt fuel","To return some exhaust gas to the intake to lower combustion temperature and NOx","To cool the intake air before it enters the engine through the manifold"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m08-l4","explain":"EGR lowers peak temperature, reducing the formation of nitrogen oxides."},
    {"id":"m08-q18","q":"What does a purge valve control?","options":["The flow of air through the idle control passage around the throttle","The flow of coolant to the heater matrix inside the cabin","The flow of oil to the camshaft timing mechanism in the cylinder head","The flow of fuel vapour from the charcoal canister to the engine"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m08-l4","explain":"The canister stores vapour from the tank, and the purge valve lets it burn."},
    {"id":"m08-q19","q":"Why should you never short an ECU output wire to ground to test a part?","options":["The wire will become magnetic and will damage other nearby sensors","The part will heat up at once and melt the connector on the engine","The battery will lose its charge far too quickly to be useful","You can destroy the driver circuit inside the ECU"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m08-l4","explain":"Use a test lamp, a meter or a scan tool actuator test instead."},
    {"id":"m08-q20","q":"Which is the best way to test an injector circuit without damaging the ECU?","options":["Connect the part straight to the battery for ten seconds and watch it","Check power and ground, measure the resistance and use an actuator test","Remove the fuse for the circuit and listen for the part to click","Short its signal wire to the engine block and see if it responds"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m08-l4","explain":"Safe tests protect the ECU and tell you where the fault is."},
    {"id":"m08-q21","q":"What does a flat oxygen sensor signal at 0.45 V mean?","options":["Open loop or a dead sensor","A rich mixture","A lean mixture","A healthy sensor in closed loop"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m08-l5","explain":"A working narrow-band sensor switches between about 0.1 and 0.9 V. A flat middle value shows that it is not producing a signal or the ECU is not in closed loop."},
    {"id":"m08-q22","q":"The downstream oxygen sensor copies the upstream sensor's pattern. What does it show?","options":["The upstream sensor has failed completely","The engine is running too cool","The converter is not storing oxygen","The fuel pressure is too high"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m08-l5","explain":"A working converter smooths the oxygen content and the rear signal is steady. A mirrored pattern means the converter has lost its capacity."},
    {"id":"m08-q23","q":"Why does an exhaust leak ahead of the oxygen sensor give a false reading?","options":["Air enters and the sensor reads lean","Exhaust gas leaves and the sensor reads rich","The sensor heater turns itself off","The sensor changes its own voltage range"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m08-l5","explain":"Leaked air adds oxygen to the sample, so the ECU thinks the mixture is lean and adds fuel, which makes the engine run rich."},
    {"id":"m08-q24","q":"Why does a wide-band sensor need a pump cell?","options":["It stores oxygen for the converter to use","It heats the exhaust gas before the turbo","It filters the fuel before the injector","It measures lambda over a wide range"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m08-l5","explain":"The ECU drives a pump current to balance the sensing cell. The current needed gives a precise lambda reading."},
    {"id":"m08-q25","q":"Which pollutant is caused mainly by a rich mixture?","options":["Oxides of nitrogen (NOx)","Water vapour","Carbon dioxide only","Carbon monoxide (CO)"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m08-l6","explain":"A rich mixture does not have enough oxygen to burn all the carbon to CO2."},
    {"id":"m08-q26","q":"Under what conditions does NOx form most?","options":["At low temperature on a cold start, when the mixture is very rich","When the mixture is very rich and the converter has not yet warmed","When the engine has just been switched off after a long drive","At high combustion temperatures, especially with a lean mixture"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m08-l6","explain":"The nitrogen and oxygen in the air combine when it is hot."},
    {"id":"m08-q27","q":"What does a three-way catalytic converter do?","options":["It adds fuel to the exhaust to improve the power at high speed","It reduces CO, HC and NOx into less harmful gases","It filters the soot from the exhaust gas as it flows out of the engine","It cools the exhaust gas to protect the silencer and the pipes behind"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m08-l6","explain":"It works best at the ideal air-fuel ratio, lambda 1."},
    {"id":"m08-q28","q":"What does a DPF do?","options":["It cools the intake air of a diesel engine before it enters the cylinders","It reduces NOx using a urea solution that is injected into the exhaust","It traps soot from a diesel engine and burns it off in regeneration","It stores the fuel vapour that comes from the tank and burns it later"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m08-l6","explain":"Regeneration burns the trapped soot to clean the filter."},
    {"id":"m08-q29","q":"What is AdBlue used for?","options":["It is injected into the exhaust of an SCR system to reduce NOx","It is added to the coolant to stop it from freezing in cold weather","It is mixed with the fuel to raise the cetane number of the diesel","It is added to the engine oil to reduce wear on the bearings and the cam"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m08-l6","explain":"SCR converts NOx to nitrogen and water using the urea solution."},
    {"id":"m08-q30","q":"Which gas is a greenhouse gas made in proportion to the fuel burned?","options":["Carbon monoxide","Carbon dioxide","Nitrogen oxides","Hydrocarbons"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m08-l6","explain":"CO2 is produced by all complete burning of fuel, so the way to reduce it is to burn less. CO is a poison and NOx and HC form smog."},
    {"id":"m08-q31","q":"A car uses 8 L/100 km of petrol. Roughly how much CO2 per 100 km is that, at 2.3 kg per litre?","options":["About 18.4 kg","About 2.3 kg per hundred kilometres","About 8 kg per hundred kilometres","About 184 kg per hundred kilometres"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m08-l6","explain":"8 litres × 2.3 kg per litre = 18.4 kg, or 184 g per kilometre."},
    {"id":"m08-q32","q":"Why must leaded petrol never be used with a catalytic converter?","options":["Lead lowers the engine compression too far","Lead gives off blue smoke at start-up","Lead makes the plugs too cold","Lead coats the catalyst and ruins it"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m08-l6","explain":"Lead forms a film on the precious metals so exhaust cannot reach them. The damage cannot be repaired."},
    {"id":"m08-q33","q":"An EGR valve is stuck open. What symptom is most likely?","options":["Knock under heavy load or hard acceleration","Rough idle or stalling","A louder exhaust note at all engine speeds","Higher power at idle and in the lower gears"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m08-l7","explain":"Exhaust gas at idle displaces air and slows combustion. Idle gets rough and the engine can stall."},
    {"id":"m08-q34","q":"What is the first check for a large EVAP leak code?","options":["The brake pads on the front wheels","The windscreen wipers and washer jets","The fuel cap and its seal","The tyre pressures on all four wheels"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m08-l7","explain":"A loose or missing cap is the most common cause of a large leak code. Check it before any other work."},
    {"id":"m08-q35","q":"Why does a DPF get blocked on a car used only for short trips?","options":["Short trips use less fuel and make less soot","It never gets hot enough to burn soot off","The filter melts from too much heat","The oil level falls so quickly"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m08-l7","explain":"Regeneration needs sustained high exhaust temperatures. Short journeys keep the filter loading, and eventually it blocks."},
    {"id":"m08-q36","q":"What is AdBlue?","options":["A fuel additive that raises the cetane number","A coolant for the diesel particulate filter","A urea solution for diesel SCR systems","A lubricant for the turbocharger bearings"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m08-l7","explain":"The urea solution turns to ammonia in the hot exhaust and converts NOx to nitrogen and water in the SCR catalyst."},
    {"id":"m08-q37","q":"What does a high HC reading show?","options":["Too much carbon dioxide in the exhaust","A fully working catalytic converter","Water in the fuel tank","Unburnt fuel, often from a misfire"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m08-l8","explain":"Hydrocarbons are unburnt fuel. High values come from misfires, rich mixtures and poor sealing."},
    {"id":"m08-q38","q":"A gas test shows high HC and high O2 with CO low. What does it suggest?","options":["A misfire: air and fuel pass through","A very rich mixture that is burning completely","A working catalytic converter at low load","A broken exhaust pipe at the silencer"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m08-l8","explain":"In a misfire the cylinder pumps out both unburnt fuel and oxygen, and CO stays low because the cylinder did not burn."},
    {"id":"m08-q39","q":"Why must the exhaust be free of leaks during an analyser test?","options":["A leak makes the probe too hot to handle safely","A leak raises the CO reading to a higher value","Air dilutes the sample, giving false readings","A leak stops the analyser from warming up properly"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m08-l8","explain":"Air entering ahead of the probe raises O2 and lowers CO2, HC and CO, giving a misleading result."},
    {"id":"m08-q40","q":"What does a flashing malfunction indicator lamp usually indicate?","options":["That the next service is due within about one month from today","A serious misfire that can damage the catalytic converter","That the air conditioning needs a refrigerant recharge before summer","A flat battery in the key fob so that it cannot lock the doors from outside"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m08-l9","explain":"Raw fuel in the converter can overheat and destroy it. Reduce the load and have it fixed."},
    {"id":"m08-q41","q":"In the code P0301, what does the first letter P stand for?","options":["Powertrain","Petrol, which is a code for faults with petrol engines only","Pressure, which is a code for faults in the tyre pressure system","Pollution, which is a code for faults in the emissions system alone"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m08-l9","explain":"P is powertrain, B is body, C is chassis and U is network."},
    {"id":"m08-q42","q":"What does a 0 as the first digit in a code such as P0301 show?","options":["A code for the transmission control system and nothing else","A manufacturer-specific code that is only valid for one make","A code for a fault in the oxygen sensor circuit only","A generic code that is the same on all vehicles"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m08-l9","explain":"A 1 as the first digit shows a manufacturer-specific code."},
    {"id":"m08-q43","q":"What is stored as freeze-frame data?","options":["The service history of the vehicle and the date of its last visit","A snapshot of sensor values at the moment the fault was recorded","The place where the vehicle was driven when the fault was stored","A complete copy of the ECU software at the time of the fault"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m08-l9","explain":"It shows the engine speed, load and temperature when the code was set."},
    {"id":"m08-q44","q":"What is true of a stored trouble code?","options":["It points to a circuit or system, not automatically to a faulty part","It always names the exact part that must be replaced to clear it","It always means that the ECU itself has failed and must be replaced","It can safely be ignored if the warning lamp on the dash is off"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m08-l9","explain":"Always test the circuit. A lean code may be caused by a vacuum leak or a dirty MAF."},
    {"id":"m08-q45","q":"Why should you record the codes before clearing them?","options":["Clearing damages the battery because the ECU draws too much current","Clearing is against the law in all countries around the world","Clearing makes the engine run in limp mode for the next few days","Clearing erases the freeze-frame data and resets the readiness monitors"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m08-l9","explain":"The information is needed to diagnose the fault, and it cannot be recovered."},
    {"id":"m08-q46","q":"A scan tool shows a lean code P0171. What is the best next step?","options":["Clear the code and hope that the lean fault does not come back","Replace the oxygen sensor immediately because it caused the code","Check for vacuum leaks, fuel pressure and the MAF sensor before replacing parts","Replace the ECU because the code points to a computer fault"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m08-l9","explain":"The code tells you the condition. Tests tell you the cause."},
    {"id":"m08-q47","q":"Which pin of the OBD-II socket is the always-live battery supply?","options":["Pin 16","Pin 4, the chassis earth connection","Pin 5, the signal earth connection","Pin 7, the K-line data connection"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m08-l9","explain":"Pin 16 is battery positive. Pins 4 and 5 are earth. CAN uses pins 6 and 14."},
    {"id":"m08-q48","q":"A scan tool will not power up on a car. What is the first check?","options":["The tyre pressures on all four of the wheels","Pin 16 for voltage, pins 4 and 5 for earth","The air filter in the airbox under the bonnet","The spark plug gap on cylinder number one"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m08-l9","explain":"The tool takes power from the socket. A blown fuse or open earth is a common cause."},
    {"id":"m08-q49","q":"The ECU has stored a lean code at 3,000 rpm and high load. What does that suggest?","options":["A vacuum leak, as it would be the same at idle","A dirty windscreen that is blocking the view","A flat spare tyre in the boot of the car","A fuel delivery problem under load"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m08-l10","explain":"A leak causes more lean at idle, where its share is large. A high-load lean points to the fuel supply: the pump, the filter or the injectors."},
    {"id":"m08-q50","q":"Why might a car be refused an emission test just after codes are cleared?","options":["The monitors are not complete","The battery has been charged too much","The tyres are the wrong size","The oil has been changed too recently"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m08-l10","explain":"Clearing codes resets the monitors. They need a proper drive cycle to complete before a test can read them."},
    {"id":"m08-q51","q":"Long-term trim is +21% at idle and +7% at 2,500 rpm, and the MAF reading at idle is normal. What is the best next step?","options":["Replace the fuel pump straight away without testing","Smoke test for a leak after the MAF sensor","Replace the MAF sensor at once without any test","Clear the codes and carry on driving the car"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m08-l10","explain":"The trim pattern shows a fixed leak that is a big share of airflow at idle. A normal MAF suggests the leak is after the sensor."},
    {"id":"m08-q52","q":"A long-term fuel trim of +20% at idle that drops to +2% at 3,000 rpm suggests what?","options":["A weak battery","A vacuum leak","A blocked exhaust","A worn camshaft"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m08-l11","explain":"A leak has a greater effect at idle, where the engine draws little air."},
    {"id":"m08-q53","q":"What can unburnt fuel from a misfire do to a catalytic converter?","options":["Burn inside it and overheat or melt it","Cool the converter down and protect it from the high exhaust heat","Make the converter work better by giving it more fuel to treat","Make the converter clean itself and last for much longer than before"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m08-l11","explain":"Repair misfires quickly to protect the converter."},
    {"id":"m08-q54","q":"Why is removing a catalytic converter a poor idea?","options":["It makes the engine run quieter and it makes the exhaust system last longer","It is illegal in most countries and increases the pollution from the vehicle","It makes the exhaust last longer and it improves the engine response","It improves the fuel economy by a large amount and helps the engine"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m08-l11","explain":"The engine is calibrated to work with the converter. Repair or replace it."},
    {"id":"m08-q55","q":"A vehicle fails an emissions test because a readiness monitor is not complete. What should you do?","options":["Clear the codes just before the test so that the lamp is off","Replace the ECU so that the monitors are all complete at once","Drive the vehicle through a suitable drive cycle so the monitors can run","Disconnect the battery for an hour so that the ECU is reset"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m08-l11","explain":"Clearing the codes resets the monitors. They need a drive cycle to complete."},
    {"id":"m08-q56","q":"A rotten-egg smell from the exhaust and loss of power suggest what?","options":["A worn clutch that slips when the engine is under a heavy load","A flat battery that is dragging the voltage down in the whole car","A failing or blocked catalytic converter","A leaking brake pipe that lets fluid drip onto the hot exhaust"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m08-l11","explain":"Sulphur compounds are processed poorly by a failing converter, and a blocked one restricts the exhaust."},
    {"id":"m08-q57","q":"A P0420 code and a recent cleared misfire. What should you do first?","options":["Fit a new converter and clear the codes at once","Remove the converter to avoid getting the code","Replace both oxygen sensors and nothing else","Fix the misfire, then check the converter"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m08-l11","explain":"A misfire overheats and destroys converters. If the cause is not fixed, a new converter will fail as well."},
    {"id":"m08-q58","q":"A reading of CO 3.5% and HC 800 ppm with normal oxygen suggests what?","options":["A lean mixture","A cold engine","A rich mixture","A diesel engine"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m08-l11","explain":"High CO and HC with little oxygen points to excess fuel. In the example the coolant sensor read very cold, so the ECU over-fuelled."}
  ],
  "cards": [
    {"id":"m08-c01","front":"What are the three steps of engine control?","back":"Inputs from sensors, processing by the ECU, outputs to actuators.","lesson":"m08-l1"},
    {"id":"m08-c02","front":"What is closed loop?","back":"The ECU corrects the mixture using the oxygen sensor signal.","lesson":"m08-l1"},
    {"id":"m08-c03","front":"What is open loop?","back":"The ECU uses stored values, usually when the engine is cold.","lesson":"m08-l1"},
    {"id":"m08-c04","front":"What is limp mode?","back":"A fail-safe mode that limits power after the ECU detects a serious fault.","lesson":"m08-l1"},
    {"id":"m08-c05","front":"What does an ECU need to work properly?","back":"A good battery supply, a switched ignition supply and several good earths.","lesson":"m08-l1"},
    {"id":"m08-c06","front":"What does an NTC thermistor do?","back":"Its resistance falls as the temperature rises.","lesson":"m08-l2"},
    {"id":"m08-c07","front":"What does a MAF sensor measure?","back":"The mass of air entering the engine.","lesson":"m08-l2"},
    {"id":"m08-c08","front":"What happens if the crankshaft sensor fails?","back":"The engine usually stops or will not start.","lesson":"m08-l2"},
    {"id":"m08-c09","front":"What does an oxygen sensor measure?","back":"The amount of oxygen left in the exhaust gas.","lesson":"m08-l2"},
    {"id":"m08-c10","front":"What does a knock sensor detect?","back":"Engine vibration caused by knock.","lesson":"m08-l2"},
    {"id":"m08-c11","front":"What are the three wires of a typical sensor?","back":"5 V reference, signal and earth.","lesson":"m08-l3"},
    {"id":"m08-c12","front":"What is a 36-1 or 60-2 wheel?","back":"A crank trigger wheel with a gap where teeth are missing, which tells the ECU the position.","lesson":"m08-l3"},
    {"id":"m08-c13","front":"Several sensors fail at once. Likely cause?","back":"A shared 5 V reference or earth fault.","lesson":"m08-l3"},
    {"id":"m08-c14","front":"What does an EGR valve do?","back":"It returns some exhaust gas to the intake to reduce NOx.","lesson":"m08-l4"},
    {"id":"m08-c15","front":"What does a purge valve do?","back":"It lets fuel vapour from the canister into the engine to burn.","lesson":"m08-l4"},
    {"id":"m08-c16","front":"Why not short an ECU output to test it?","back":"You can destroy the driver circuit in the ECU.","lesson":"m08-l4"},
    {"id":"m08-c17","front":"Normal narrow-band oxygen sensor signal?","back":"Switches between about 0.1 V (lean) and 0.9 V (rich), one or two times a second.","lesson":"m08-l5"},
    {"id":"m08-c18","front":"Upstream and downstream oxygen sensor jobs?","back":"Upstream controls fuel. Downstream monitors the catalyst. A mirrored downstream signal means the catalyst is failing.","lesson":"m08-l5"},
    {"id":"m08-c19","front":"What does a wide-band sensor measure?","back":"The actual air-fuel ratio over a wide range, using a pump cell.","lesson":"m08-l5"},
    {"id":"m08-c20","front":"What causes CO?","back":"A rich mixture with too little oxygen.","lesson":"m08-l6"},
    {"id":"m08-c21","front":"What causes NOx?","back":"High combustion temperatures.","lesson":"m08-l6"},
    {"id":"m08-c22","front":"What does a three-way catalyst do?","back":"It reduces CO, HC and NOx into less harmful gases.","lesson":"m08-l6"},
    {"id":"m08-c23","front":"What does a DPF do?","back":"It traps diesel soot and burns it off in regeneration.","lesson":"m08-l6"},
    {"id":"m08-c24","front":"What is AdBlue?","back":"A urea solution used by SCR systems to reduce NOx.","lesson":"m08-l6"},
    {"id":"m08-c25","front":"CO2 per litre of petrol and diesel?","back":"About 2.3 kg for petrol and 2.7 kg for diesel.","lesson":"m08-l6"},
    {"id":"m08-c26","front":"What does a stuck-open EGR valve cause?","back":"Rough idle, stalling and hesitation.","lesson":"m08-l7"},
    {"id":"m08-c27","front":"How does EVAP work?","back":"Vapour is stored in a charcoal canister, and the ECU purges it into the intake when the engine is warm.","lesson":"m08-l7"},
    {"id":"m08-c28","front":"Three types of DPF regeneration?","back":"Passive (long hot drive), active (ECU adds late fuel) and forced (scan tool, by a technician).","lesson":"m08-l7"},
    {"id":"m08-c29","front":"What is AdBlue and when does it freeze?","back":"A 32.5% urea solution injected before the SCR catalyst. It freezes at about −11 °C.","lesson":"m08-l7"},
    {"id":"m08-c30","front":"Healthy gas readings with a working catalyst?","back":"CO under 0.5%, HC under 100 ppm, CO2 13.5 to 15.5%, O2 under 0.5%.","lesson":"m08-l8"},
    {"id":"m08-c31","front":"High HC and high O2 with low CO?","back":"A misfire.","lesson":"m08-l8"},
    {"id":"m08-c32","front":"How deep should the probe go?","back":"At least 30 cm into the tailpipe, or as the maker says.","lesson":"m08-l8"},
    {"id":"m08-c33","front":"What does a flashing MIL mean?","back":"A serious misfire that can damage the catalytic converter.","lesson":"m08-l9"},
    {"id":"m08-c34","front":"What do the letters P, B, C, U mean in a code?","back":"Powertrain, body, chassis, network.","lesson":"m08-l9"},
    {"id":"m08-c35","front":"What does a 0 as the first digit in a code mean?","back":"A generic code, the same on all vehicles.","lesson":"m08-l9"},
    {"id":"m08-c36","front":"What is freeze-frame data?","back":"A snapshot of sensor values when the fault was stored.","lesson":"m08-l9"},
    {"id":"m08-c37","front":"Why record codes before clearing them?","back":"Clearing erases freeze-frame data and resets the readiness monitors.","lesson":"m08-l9"},
    {"id":"m08-c38","front":"What does a code point to?","back":"A circuit or system, not automatically to a bad part.","lesson":"m08-l9"},
    {"id":"m08-c39","front":"OBD-II socket pins 4, 5, 16, 6 and 14?","back":"4 chassis earth, 5 signal earth, 16 battery positive, 6 and 14 CAN high and low.","lesson":"m08-l9"},
    {"id":"m08-c40","front":"What is a freeze frame?","back":"A snapshot of the engine conditions when a fault was stored.","lesson":"m08-l10"},
    {"id":"m08-c41","front":"What is a drive cycle?","back":"A pattern of driving that lets the ECU complete its monitor tests.","lesson":"m08-l10"},
    {"id":"m08-c42","front":"What does a high positive fuel trim mean?","back":"The ECU is adding fuel to correct a lean condition.","lesson":"m08-l11"},
    {"id":"m08-c43","front":"What does a lean trim that disappears at higher speed suggest?","back":"A vacuum leak.","lesson":"m08-l11"},
    {"id":"m08-c44","front":"Why is removing a catalyst illegal?","back":"It increases pollution and the engine is calibrated to use it.","lesson":"m08-l11"}
  ]
});
